        /// <summary>Delete an account</summary>
        /// <remarks>Asks QuickBooks to delete an account (qbXML ListDel). QuickBooks refuses to delete accounts that transactions or other records still use (<c>409 QBD_OBJECT_IN_USE</c>). While the company file is open in multi-user mode, QuickBooks can accept the delete but keep the account, inactive and readable by its ID; single-user mode permits removal, but success depends on the record type and its dependencies. Customer removal was verified on Enterprise 24. The API reads the account back in the same request, so <c>deleted</c> is true only when QuickBooks no longer returns it; otherwise <c>deleted</c> is false, <c>deactivated</c> and <c>isActive</c> say what QuickBooks kept, and <c>retainedBecause</c> is <c>multi_user_mode</c> when that was the reason. Treat a kept account as not deleted: it still counts as a name in use. <c>deleted</c> is null when the read-back failed (warning <c>QBD_DELETE_NOT_VERIFIED</c>); QuickBooks accepted the delete, but removal is unverified, so inspect the record before making another write. Prefer deactivating: updating the account with <c>isActive: false</c> keeps its history and hides it from lists. Send <c>confirm=true</c> to show the deletion is intended; without it the API answers <c>400 INVALID_PARAMETER</c> and sends nothing. QuickBooks lists deleted objects through the deleted-list-objects list for 90 days. Send an <c>Idempotency-Key</c> to make retries safe. Operation <c>qbd.accounts.delete</c>: <c>DELETE /v1/quickbooks-desktop/accounts/{id}</c>. <c>Enqueue.DeleteAsync</c> runs it in async mode.</remarks>
