        /// <summary>Async-mode variants of this resource's operations: each sends <c>Prefer: respond-async</c> and returns a <see cref="RequestHandle{T}"/> instead of waiting for QuickBooks.</summary>
        public QbdEnqueue Enqueue { get; }

        /// <summary>Run several operations in one round trip</summary>
        /// <remarks>Runs up to 25 typed QuickBooks Desktop operations together: they go to QuickBooks as one qbXML message set in one Web Connector round trip, in order, and the response holds one typed result per member. Each member is validated exactly like its own endpoint before anything is queued; an invalid member fails the whole call with <c>400</c> and <c>param</c> naming it (for example <c>requests[2].body.name</c>). With <c>onError: stopOnError</c> (the default) QuickBooks skips every member after the first member it answers with an error (<c>not_processed</c>). A member QuickBooks answers with a warning, such as a query for an ID that does not exist, does not stop the batch, even when its result is <c>failed</c>. With <c>continueOnError</c> every member runs. A list member runs as one query of at most <c>limit</c> records (<c>cursor</c> is not accepted). A read-only key may batch reads only. Writes: send one <c>Idempotency-Key</c> for the whole batch. If the response from QuickBooks is lost, the call fails with <c>QBD_WRITE_OUTCOME_UNKNOWN</c> and <c>details.members</c> lists every member: each write member is <c>unknown</c> (never resend it blindly), and a read member never proves that a write applied. Billing counts the batch like the same requests sent one by one. See [Batch requests](https://www.desktopaccountingapi.com/docs/guides/batch/). Operation <c>qbd.batch</c>: <c>POST /v1/quickbooks-desktop/batch</c>. <c>Enqueue.BatchAsync</c> runs it in async mode.</remarks>
